## Why This Question Is Asked
Assesses the ability to design a constraint solver for 3-valued Kleene logic, demonstrating handling of partial knowledge and undefined states in logical policies.

## Key Concepts
- 3-valued truth tables for ¬, ∨, ∧, and →
- Arc-consistency enforcement on ternary domains
- Fixed-point propagation without backtracking

## Implementation
```javascript
const order = { 'F': 0, 'U': 1, 'T': 2 };

function not(v) {
  return v === 'F' ? 'T' : v === 'T' ? 'F' : 'U';
}

function max(a, b) {
  return order[a] > order[b] ? a : b;
}

function implies(a, b) {
  return max(not(a), b);
}

function supports(constraint, varIndex, value, currentAssignment) {
  const vars = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'];
  for (const val of ['F', 'U', 'T']) {
    currentAssignment[vars[varIndex]] = val;
    if (constraint(currentAssignment) !== 'F') return true;
  }
  return false;
}

function arcConsistency(domains, constraints) {
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const constraint of constraints) {
      for (const [varName, domain] of Object.entries(domains)) {
        const filtered = domain.filter(val => 
          supports(constraint, varName, val, { A: domains.A[0], B: domains.B[0], C: domains.C[0], D: domains.D[0] })
        );
        if (filtered.length !== domain.length) {
          domains[varName] = filtered;
          changed = true;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return domains;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests design of tri-valued constraint systems with a custom operator and backtracking propagation, ensuring candidates reason about Kleene semantics and propagation rules.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic with T > U > F; ∧/∨ act as min/max.
- Custom operator Nand defined as not(and(x,y)).
- Backtracking propagation to prune domains and find a satisfying assignment.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Basic tri-valued lattice: T > U > F
const rank = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ return rank[a] <= rank[b] ? a : b; }
function or(a,b){ return rank[a] >= rank[b] ? a : b; }
function nand(a,b){ return not(and(a,b)); }
function implies(p,q){ return or(not(p), q); }

// sample assignment
let F1='T', F2='F', F3='U', F4='F';
console.log(implies(or(F1,F2), F3)); // U
console.log(and(F2,F4)); // F
console.log(nand(F3,F4)); // T
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to N flags and arbitrary constraint sets?
- What changes when moving to a 4-valued logic or alternative lattices?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests reasoning with three-valued logic in policy-like constraints, ensuring candidates can propagate values and avoid false evaluations when handling uncertainty.

## Key Concepts
- Three-valued logic with T/F/U truth values
- Implication defined as ¬X ∨ Y
- Constraint propagation and backtracking in constraint satisfaction problems
- Handling unknown values without forcing false evaluations

## Code Example
```javascript
function implies(X,Y){
  const not = {T:'F',F:'T',U:'U'};
  const max = (a,b)=> (a==='T'||b==='T')?'T':(a==='U'||b==='U')?'U':'F';
  return max(not[X], Y);
}
```

Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design and reason about 3-valued constraint problems, including propagation and backtracking, in a realistic policy-like setting.

Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic semantics
- Constraint propagation and backtracking search
- Minimal use of Unknowns to satisfy all constraints
- Concrete construction to validate satisfiability

Code Example
```javascript
function propagate3Valued(vars) {
  // vars: {Ingest:'T'|'F'|'U', Normalize:'T'|'F'|'U', Train:'T'|'F'|'U', Serve:'T'|'F'|'U'}
  // Simple forward-propagation stub for the three constraints
  // Returns updated map ensuring no explicit F is forced if possible
  // (This is illustrative; real impl would loop until fixed point)
  const v = { ...vars };
  // If (A ∨ B) → C and A is T or B is T, then C must be T
  if ((v.Ingest === 'T' || v.Normalize === 'T') && v.Train !== 'T') v.Train = 'T';
  // If (A ∨ B) → C and C is F, then both A and B must be F
  if (v.Train === 'F' && (v.Ingest !== 'F' || v.Normalize !== 'F')) { v.Ingest = 'F'; v.Normalize = 'F'; }
  // If (D ∨ E) → F and F is F, then D and E must be F
  if (v.Serve === 'F' && (v.Train !== 'F' || v.Ingest !== 'F')) { v.Train = 'F'; v.Ingest = 'F'; }
  return v;
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to 8 variables with more complex constraints?  
- How would you handle non-monotone constraints or introduce probabilistic uncertainty in truth values?
